WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.457 --> 00:00:02.745
- I'm gonna win.

00:00:02.745 --> 00:00:04.318
- No you're not.

00:00:04.318 --> 00:00:05.636
- [Voiceover] Remember “Finding Nemo,”

00:00:05.636 --> 00:00:08.593
the movie that introduced us to clownfish?

00:00:08.593 --> 00:00:09.950
- Love you, dad.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.255
- [Voiceover] Dory, the
absent-minded sidekick,

00:00:12.255 --> 00:00:15.327
now has her own movie, but
marine conservationists,

00:00:15.327 --> 00:00:16.780
they're not excited.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.530
They're actually kind of worried.

00:00:22.154 --> 00:00:24.890
This right here is a blue
tang, the real-life Dory.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.649
They only live on coral
reefs in the south Pacific.

00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:30.094
But before we get to her story,

00:00:30.094 --> 00:00:32.435
let's talk about Nemo, the clownfish.

00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:34.200
When “Finding Nemo” came out...

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.933
Well, okay, I'll let Karen tell you.

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:37.470
- I'm Karen Burke Da Silva.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.901
I'm a marine biologist and
a conservation biologist

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:42.989
here at Flinders University
of South Australia.

00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:44.946
- [Voiceover] So what
happened with “Finding Nemo?”

00:00:44.946 --> 00:00:47.036
- After the release of “Finding Nemo,”

00:00:47.036 --> 00:00:50.190
we found that there was
quite a big increase

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:54.332
in the number of people that
wanted a clownfish as a pet.

00:00:54.332 --> 00:00:57.527
With the increase in demand, it did have

00:00:57.527 --> 00:00:59.662
an increase in captive breeding,

00:00:59.662 --> 00:01:02.267
but it also meant that more fish

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.214
were being taken from
the wild, and in fact,

00:01:05.214 --> 00:01:08.494
some places there's been
so much over-collecting

00:01:08.494 --> 00:01:11.186
that clownfish have gone locally extinct.

00:01:11.186 --> 00:01:13.494
- [Voiceover] Oh, so captive breeding

00:01:13.494 --> 00:01:16.304
seems like the way to go.

00:01:16.304 --> 00:01:17.770
Back to Dory, the blue tang.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.066
If her movie has the
same effect as Nemo's,

00:01:20.066 --> 00:01:23.179
moviegoers are sure to want to
buy a blue tang of their own.

00:01:23.179 --> 00:01:24.381
- But there's a problem.

00:01:24.381 --> 00:01:25.788
- [Voiceover] And you are?

00:01:25.788 --> 00:01:27.786
- My name is Matt DiMaggio.

00:01:27.786 --> 00:01:30.806
I'm an assistant professor
at the University of Florida

00:01:30.806 --> 00:01:32.433
Tropical Aquaculture Laboratory.

00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:35.247
- [Voiceover] Ah, okay,
Matt, so what's the problem?

00:01:35.247 --> 00:01:38.136
- Clownfish are relatively
easy to raise in captivity.

00:01:38.136 --> 00:01:41.046
But up until now, no one has
raised blue tangs in captivity.

00:01:41.046 --> 00:01:44.544
Blue tang larvae are
inherently difficult to raise.

00:01:44.544 --> 00:01:47.443
They have a very small
mouth when they hatch out,

00:01:47.443 --> 00:01:50.005
so the food particles
that we have to offer them

00:01:50.005 --> 00:01:53.535
are much smaller and much
more difficult to raise.

00:01:53.535 --> 00:01:55.515
The best success we've had to date

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:59.094
is using a zooplankton called copepod.

00:01:59.094 --> 00:02:03.491
The copepods that we grow here
only eat live micro-algae,

00:02:03.491 --> 00:02:05.921
so you now have to grow live micro-algae

00:02:05.921 --> 00:02:07.787
to feed to the copepods.

00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:09.933
- [Voiceover] Basically,
raising blue tangs so far

00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:13.887
is really, really hard, which means...

00:02:13.887 --> 00:02:17.002
- 100% of blue tang are
currently being taken

00:02:17.002 --> 00:02:19.533
from the wild for the aquarium trade.

00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:22.038
Most collectors in Australia will use

00:02:22.038 --> 00:02:24.700
very careful collecting measures.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.287
In other parts of the
world, southeast Asia,

00:02:27.287 --> 00:02:32.116
they are using cyanide still
to collect aquarium fish.

00:02:32.116 --> 00:02:35.435
- [Voiceover] Hmmm, that's not
good, but there's hope.

00:02:35.435 --> 00:02:37.186
Matt and his team are working hard

00:02:37.186 --> 00:02:38.899
to breed blue tangs successfully.

00:02:38.899 --> 00:02:41.112
- There's only one other tang
species, the yellow tang,

00:02:41.112 --> 00:02:43.090
that's ever been raised
in captivity before.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.451
We're hoping that the blue
tang will be the second.

00:02:46.451 --> 00:02:47.771
- [Voiceover] Alright, so until they do,

00:02:47.771 --> 00:02:49.991
when little Johnny says he wants a Dory,

00:02:49.991 --> 00:02:52.471
perhaps the better
choice, for now at least,

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:56.916
is to stick to the blue
betta… or a goldfish.

00:02:56.916 --> 00:02:59.416
(light music)

